---
/**
 * The configure page (T-018, FR-025 – FR-035).
 *
 * Three setup steps in the order an operator performs them, each with the panel
 * surface that owns it; the accounts disclaimer's four facts *before* the field
 * they govern; the per-account expected-login constraint; the Settings field
 * list, generated from the service's own declaration rather than retyped; what
 * the two controls that delete will delete, stated before the operator reaches
 * them; where the document lives; the layered starting prompt; and the negative
 * fact that closes the page — there is no other configuration surface.
 *
 * `.astro` rather than `.md` for the reason `install.astro` sets out: a
 * markdown page cannot render the generated field list.
 *
 * `/v1/config` appears once, here, because FR-033 is the requirement that names
 * it. No other page names an endpoint (product owner's Q3 ruling).
 */
import Layout from '../layout.astro';
import SettingsFields from '../components/settings-fields.astro';
import { withBase } from '../data/site.ts';
---

<Layout title="Configure">
    <h1>Configure</h1>

    <p>
        Three steps stand between an installed extension and work arriving, in this order: register the
        projects, add a GitHub account, bind a repository. Everything after them — the service
        configuration, the starting prompt — is optional and comes later.
    </p>

    <h2>1. Register the project, in OpenChamber</h2>

    <p>
        Each repository you will bind needs an OpenChamber project registered for it, one per repository.
        No panel surface owns this step, because the extension cannot do it: there is no project-creation
        call anywhere in it. OpenChamber registers a project by the command palette's <em>Add project</em>,
        the sidebar's add button, or the folder browser.
    </p>

    <h2>2. Add a GitHub account, on the Accounts tab</h2>

    <p>Read the disclaimer first. It is always visible under the account list, and its four facts are:</p>

    <ul>
        <li>
            Your token is sent to a <strong>local service</strong>, which is allowed but
            sandbox-advisory: an allowed service has your full user access — it can run any command and
            read or write any file your user can.
        </li>
        <li>
            The token is stored <strong>outside OpenChamber extension storage</strong>, protected by file
            permissions you can back up — and <strong>unencrypted, in plaintext on disk</strong>,
            readable by anything running as your user.
        </li>
        <li>
            A connection is recorded in the service audit as an <strong>occurrence only</strong>: an
            identity and a time, never the token.
        </li>
        <li>
            There is <strong>nothing to accept or decline</strong>. It is information, not a consent
            step, and it has no control to press.
        </li>
    </ul>

    <p>
        Then paste a <strong>fine-grained, read-only</strong> personal access token into
        the <em>GitHub token</em> field and choose <strong>Connect account</strong>.
        The token needs <em>Metadata: read</em>, <em>Issues: read</em>, and <em>Pull requests: read</em>,
        plus <em>Contents: read</em> only if you pass repository metadata to OpenChamber, and <strong>no write scopes</strong>
        — an organization approval is needed where your organization requires one.
        The token exists only in transit: it is never written to panel state, extension storage, logs, or the
        audit trail. A success reads <code>Connected as &lt;your login&gt;</code>.
    </p>

    <p>
        The one field beside the paste is <strong>Expected GitHub login</strong>, optional. It is
        supplied where the account is created and is the per-account constraint: leave it empty and the
        account is added with no login constraint; supply a value, and a token whose identity disagrees
        with it is <strong>refused rather than applied</strong> — so a token pasted into the wrong slot
        cannot become an account under the wrong identity.
    </p>

    <h2>3. Bind a repository, on the Bindings tab</h2>

    <p>
        Choose <strong>Add binding</strong> and fill the form: <em>Repository (owner/name)</em>, the
        account to <strong>Poll as account</strong>, and the project to dispatch into, picked from the
        existing projects. If your project is not in the list, the <em>Not listed?</em> line under the
        picker tells you to register it in OpenChamber first — the extension will not create one. Then
        tick the triggers you want: <strong>Assignment</strong>, <strong>Mention</strong>,
        and <strong>Review request</strong>. The mention token in force is derived from the bound
        account's login and matches case-insensitively; the allowed-users field beside it restricts which logins
        may trigger dispatches from that repository, and a dispatch from anyone else is refused rather
        than run.
    </p>

    <p>
        <strong>Save changes</strong> writes the binding, and polling picks it up within one interval —
        60 seconds by default, and every field below can change that. Triggers that hit the same issue
        coalesce into one dispatch, so one issue produces one unit of work however many references it
        collects.
    </p>

    <h2>Settings: the single configuration input</h2>

    <p>
        The <strong>Settings</strong> tab is the only place the whole service configuration is edited,
        and the panel makes no verdict of its own. <strong>Save configuration</strong> writes the entire
        document in one go, and the <strong>service</strong> is the only validator: an out-of-range
        value is sent there and refused there, in an answer naming the field and the remediation, rather
        than being blocked in the panel. <strong>Discard changes</strong> returns every field to the last
        values read.
    </p>

    <SettingsFields />

    <p>
        Every row also carries the current value and its unit. The <code>expectedAgent</code> row is the
        agent-verification baseline, and it is <strong>blank by default</strong>: blank means no baseline
        is configured, so after each dispatch the service records which agent actually ran and compares
        nothing. Set it to the same agent you have pinned as OpenChamber's Default Agent and the panel
        warns, after every dispatch, when a session reports a different one. It warns and never blocks —
        the session still runs.
    </p>

    <h2>Before you lower a retention limit, or restore the defaults</h2>

    <p>
        Two controls delete history, and both arm a two-step confirmation that states the consequence
        before you act on it. The consequences differ, so they are stated separately.
    </p>

    <h3>Lowering a retention limit</h3>

    <p>
        Three fields are retention limits: <code>auditRetentionDays</code> (how far back the audit history
        reaches), <code>auditMaxEntries</code> (the cap on it), and <code>excerptRetentionDays</code> (how
        long stored payload excerpts are kept). Lowering any of them arms a confirmation naming, per
        field, the limit moving from its current value to the proposed one, and the rule that follows:
        entries older than the new day window will be deleted at the next trim pass; the oldest
        unprotected entries beyond the new cap will be removed at the next trim pass; stored payload
        excerpts older than the new day window will be cleared at the next trim pass. Saving then takes a
        second activation of the same control, and <strong>Cancel</strong> returns every field to the
        last values read.
    </p>

    <p>
        <strong>When:</strong> at the next trim pass — the poll-cycle boundary after the change is in
        force, and once at service start. The save itself deletes nothing; the pass does, on the next
        boundary.
    </p>

    <p>
        <strong>What survives:</strong> the rows that keep a dispatch explainable are never removed — the
        row that opens its chain, the row that records its outcome, account rows, binding rows, and
        decision rows — and the trim appends an <code>audit.trimmed</code> row recording exactly what it
        took. Under the entry cap specifically, nothing protected is removed to satisfy the cap: the
        history may exceed the cap by exactly the protected set that was kept.
    </p>

    <p>
        <strong>Raising a limit deletes nothing</strong>, and arms no confirmation. Trimming is
        irreversible: there is no restore path for it.
    </p>

    <h3>Restoring the defaults</h3>

    <p>
        <strong>Restore defaults</strong> always arms, because it writes the documented defaults for the
        whole document. The confirmation names every field the write will change, as <code>current → default</code>,
        in the service's own field order — so you can see the exact scope
        before it happens — and it carries the deletion block above for any retention limit the defaults
        happen to <em>lower</em>, because a default that trims more than your current setting deletes. A
        second activation writes; <strong>Cancel</strong> returns every field to the last values read. If
        every field already matches its default, the write would change nothing and the confirmation says
        so.
    </p>

    <h2>Where the configuration lives</h2>

    <p>
        In <code>config.json</code>, inside the service data directory that <strong>Status</strong> prints
        as its <em>Data directory</em> line — <code>~/.config/openchamber/mecha-turk/</code> by default.
        The directory is created <code>0700</code> and every file the service writes is <code>0600</code>,
        so the document is readable only by your own account and yours to back up. It
        is a plain JSON file, which you can edit by hand and keep in a backup alongside your SSH keys.
    </p>

    <p>
        A hand-edited document that fails validation is <strong>set aside</strong> — moved aside with a
        warning naming the quarantine — and the documented defaults take over until you save a valid
        document through the tab, which says so in the line above the rows. A document written before a
        field existed is not treated that way: the missing field takes its default and the rest of your
        values are kept.
    </p>

    <p>
        <strong>There is no other way to configure this.</strong> No environment file, no environment
        variable, and no manifest setting configures anything — the Settings tab and the <code>GET</code>/<code>PUT /v1/config</code> endpoints
        behind it are the whole surface. The environment variables the service does read are the host's
        own loopback port and bearer token,
        which it requires and never offers as configuration.
    </p>

    <h2>The starting prompt, in three tiers</h2>

    <p>
        A dispatch can open its session with <strong>your</strong> sentence. The starting prompt is a
        block of operator text delivered to the agent verbatim as the first thing it reads, above the
        automatic framing Mecha Turk builds from the event. It is configuration, not a template, and it
        exists at exactly three tiers — one per surface, each with one field:
    </p>

    <ul>
        <li>
            <strong>Global</strong> — every dispatch the service detects. Set it on the <code>startingPrompt</code> row
            in <strong>Settings</strong>, in force for events detected
            from the next poll on; queued work keeps the snapshot it was queued with.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Account</strong> — every dispatch polled by that GitHub account. Set it on that
            account's <em>Starting prompt for dispatches from this account</em> field on <strong>Accounts</strong>;
            the account's <strong>Save changes</strong> writes it, and leaving
            a member out of that save leaves its stored value alone.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Binding</strong> — every dispatch from that repository binding. Set it in the binding
            editor's <em>Starting prompt for dispatches from this repository</em> field, written by the
            editor's own <strong>Save changes</strong>.
        </li>
    </ul>

    <p>
        <strong>The tiers stack; they never replace one another.</strong> The order is fixed and most
        general first — <strong>global → account → binding</strong> — with exactly one blank line between
        consecutive <em>set</em> tiers, all of it inside a single <code>--- BEGIN OPERATOR STARTING PROMPT ---</code> fence,
        with the automatic event frame beneath it. An <strong>unset tier contributes nothing at all</strong>: no empty line, no substitute text,
        no note about its absence. A dispatch whose three tiers are all unset therefore carries no fence
        and is byte-identical to what Mecha Turk sent before the field existed.
    </p>

    <p>
        The delivered message carries <strong>no tier labels</strong>. Which tiers produced a dispatch is
        recorded on that dispatch's row and in the audit as <code>promptSources</code> — <code>global</code>, <code>account</code>, <code>binding</code>
        — never read back out of the text.
        No default is invented at any tier: layering is the stacking of your text, never the product's
        own.
    </p>

    <h3>What the field guarantees</h3>

    <p>The same rules apply at every tier, enforced by one validator at all three save paths:</p>

    <ul>
        <li>
            <strong>The text is literal.</strong> There is no substitution: <code>{'{number}'}</code>, <code>$var</code>,
            and <code>%s</code> arrive as exactly those characters.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>It cannot select an agent.</strong> The session runs your pinned Default Agent, no
            wording in the prompt changes that, and the post-dispatch read-back still reports the agent
            the session actually ran under.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>2,000 characters</strong> (Unicode code points) after trimming, per tier. A longer
            value is refused naming the field and the cap — never truncated silently — and a refusal at
            one tier leaves the other two stored values untouched.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>A credential-shaped value is refused, not stored.</strong> If the text looks like a
            token, an <code>Authorization:</code> header, or a bearer credential, the save is refused and
            the previously stored prompt stays in force. The rejected value appears nowhere: not in the
            file, not in a log, not in an audit row.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Reserved markers are refused.</strong> A line beginning <code>--- BEGIN </code> or <code>--- END </code> would
            imitate the composition's own containment structure, so it is
            refused rather than delivered. Those two prefixes are the whole rule, so a marker added later
            is covered without a new one.
        </li>
    </ul>

    <p>
        That is the complete set of refusals — length, credential shape, reserved marker, and
        well-formedness. There is no content policy: what you say to your own agent is your own business.
        Each surface shows an explicit <strong>not set</strong> state while its tier is empty, never an
        empty box that reads as an instruction the agent will receive, and clearing a field unsets <strong>that tier only</strong>.
    </p>

    <p>
        Each change writes one audit row for its tier — a fingerprint and a length, never the text — so you
        can tell which prompts were in force without the prompt being recorded anywhere an audit reader
        could quote it. A dispatch keeps the snapshot it was queued with, so a retry composes the same
        message and an edit never changes queued work.
    </p>

    <p>
        Reading a dispatch row, its states, and the controls that move them is on the <a href={withBase('/use/')}>use page</a>;
        when one of them does not go through, the <a href={withBase('/debug/')}>debug page</a> has the symptom table.
    </p>
</Layout>